<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paolo Uccello</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a>–<a href="/years/1475">1475</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paolo Uccello (1397–1475) was a Florentine painter known for his fascination with linear perspective. His works include the painted equestrian monument to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hawkwood">John Hawkwood</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a> of 1436 and the three panels of the Battle of San Romano, now divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in which foreshortened figures and broken lances show his experiments with depth.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paolo-uccello.jpg" alt="Paolo Uccello" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Florentine School, Unknown Master, Italian (active late 15th century); généralement attribuée à Paolo Uccello · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cinq_ma%C3%AEtres_de_la_Renaissance_florentine_-_Uccello_(Louvre).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The sources for his life are few: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-vasari">Giorgio Vasari</a>'s biography, written 75 years after his death, and a few contemporary official documents. Born Paolo di Dono, he was probably born in Pratovecchio, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>, the hometown of his father Dono di Paolo, a barber-surgeon. His mother, Antonia, came from a high-born Florentine family. His nickname Uccello, &quot;little bird&quot;, came from his fondness for painting birds.</p><p>From 1412 until 1416 he was apprenticed to the sculptor Lorenzo Ghiberti, creator of the doors of the Florence Baptistery, whose workshop was then the premier centre of Florentine art. It was around this time that his lifelong friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatello">Donatello</a> began. He was admitted to the painters' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, the Compagnia di San Luca, in 1414, and joined the Arte dei Medici e degli Speziali in 1415.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>By 1424 Uccello was earning his own living. That year he painted episodes of the Creation and the Fall for the Green Cloister of Santa Maria Novella in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, followed by scenes from the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a>, which brought him great fame. In 1425 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to work on the mosaics for the façade of San Marco, all since lost, and he was back in Florence in 1431. In 1443 he painted the figures on the clock of the Duomo and designed stained glass windows for it.</p><p>Around the mid-1450s he painted the three panels of the Battle of San Romano for the Palazzo Medici, commemorating the Florentine victory over the Sienese in 1432. Their foreshortened forms, extending in many planes, give a controlled structure to the chaos of battle. From 1465 to 1469 he worked in Urbino with his son Donato for the Confraternity of Corpus Domini, painting the predella of its altarpiece, the Miracle of the Profaned Host.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his Florentine tax return of August 1469 Uccello declared that he was old and ailing, his wife was ill, and he could no longer work. He made his testament on 11 November 1475 and died on 10 December at the hospital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, aged 78. He was buried in his father's tomb in the church of Santo Spirito.</p><p>Vasari wrote that Uccello was obsessed with perspective and would stay up all night in his study trying to grasp the exact vanishing point. He worked in the Late Gothic tradition, emphasising colour and pageantry, and his style was idiosyncratic; he left no school of followers. His perspective influenced painters such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-della-francesca">Piero della Francesca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-durer">Albrecht Dürer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paolo Uccello” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the fourteenth century the republic relied increasingly on hired companies under condottieri such as the Englishman John Hawkwood, commemorated by <b>Paolo Uccello</b>’s fresco in the cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Pratovecchio, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.79,11.72&amp;year=1447" aria-label="Pratovecchio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4832 612 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paolo Uccello"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5282 882h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5282 882h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.79,11.72&amp;year=1447">What was here in 1447? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
